1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,500
Puppy-dog eyes are
anatomy, not just charm.

2
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:06,569
That inner-brow lift comes from
a small muscle: the levator

3
00:00:06,569 --> 00:00:10,500
anguli oculi medialis, above
the inner corner of the eye.

4
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:13,700
In 2019, researchers dissected
four wolves and six dogs.

5
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:16,100
The muscle was consistently present in the

6
00:00:16,100 --> 00:00:18,500
dogs they examined, and not in the wolves.

7
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:23,625
Dogs also produced the eyebrow movement
more often and more intensely than wolves.

8
00:00:23,625 --> 00:00:26,500
The highest intensities
appeared only in dogs.

9
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:29,500
The team suggests this may have given dogs

10
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:32,714
an edge: a face that
pulls at human sympathy.

11
00:00:32,714 --> 00:00:35,500
It is a hypothesis, not a proven cause.

12
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:40,640
What is clear is that the muscle is part
of dog anatomy, not a human invention.

13
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:42,267
And the look works on us.

14
00:00:42,267 --> 00:00:45,000
Evolution shaped the plea; we answered it.
